1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,781
Prompt caching: you think your 100-page PDF costs you once, but without it, 30 questions actually cost you full price 30 times.

2
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:10,017
Here's why.

3
00:00:10,317 --> 00:00:13,898
Without caching, it's a student re-reading the whole book per question.

4
00:00:14,198 --> 00:00:17,083
Question one: all one hundred pages, full price.

5
00:00:17,383 --> 00:00:18,807
Question two: all again.

6
00:00:19,107 --> 00:00:21,471
By question thirty: paid thirty times.

7
00:00:21,971 --> 00:00:26,649
With prompt caching, question one pays a quarter more, by default, to save its work.

8
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:32,499
Questions two to thirty reuse that work for about a tenth on most models, and usually answer sooner.

9
00:00:32,999 --> 00:00:33,434
How?

10
00:00:33,734 --> 00:00:38,662
The document goes first, the same every time; only the new question is read fresh.

11
00:00:39,162 --> 00:00:43,471
OpenAI's math: ten requests cost just over two full reads, not ten.

12
00:00:43,771 --> 00:00:45,487
Ours for thirty: about four.

13
00:00:45,787 --> 00:00:48,315
That's how you stop paying full price thirty times.

14
00:00:48,815 --> 00:00:53,519
The catch: on Claude, unused saved work can fade after five minutes, by default.

15
00:00:53,819 --> 00:00:55,958
Same book: read once, reuse the work.

16
00:00:56,258 --> 00:00:57,660
Subscribe for the next one.
